GitHub is a web-based platform used for storing, sharing, and managing code. It’s built around a tool called Git, which is a version control system.

Here’s a simple way to understand it:

🔹 What GitHub is
GitHub is like an online workspace for developers.
It lets you save your code, track changes, and work with others on projects.
🔹 What Git is (important!)
Git is the system that keeps track of changes in your code.
It remembers every version, so you can go back if something breaks.
🔹 Key features of GitHub
Repositories (Repos)
A project folder that contains your code and files.
Example: a website, app, or program.
Version Control
Every change you make is saved.
You can compare versions or restore old ones.
Collaboration
Multiple people can work on the same project.
Developers can suggest changes using pull requests.
Branches
You can create separate versions of your project to test new ideas without breaking the main code.
Open Source
Many projects are public, so anyone can view or contribute.
